#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""boundary-gate: refuse anything in this repository that is not public.

This repository holds the engines, the specification, the public model modules
and the packs written from public standards. Packs and model modules written
from distribution-controlled or vendor documents live in a private overlay
repository (docs/boundary.md). This gate reads every file git would commit and
exits 1 on any finding. A file it cannot read is a finding, and so is a tree
with no files.

Checks:
  marking     every model/*.def and packs/*/pack.def says `distribution public`
              exactly once, has at least one `source` line and no second
              `module` or `format` line; a .def file stands nowhere else,
              except the fixtures in packs/conformance/tool-fixtures/ and
              tools/gen/tests/fixtures/, which state no other marking
  closure     every `uses` names a module in model/, and every mapped key is
              declared by its module
  filetype    every file has an allowed extension (no documents, spreadsheets,
              archives or binaries), has a UTF-8 name, is UTF-8 text with no
              NUL byte, and an .svg holds no script, no embedded data and no
              reference outside the file
  phrase      no text and no file path carries a handling marking of a
              restricted document (distribution statements B to F,
              export-control and proprietary banners)
  hygiene     no text carries a local absolute path under a home directory,
              the name of a private organization, or an email address
  needle      with --needles FILE: no text and no file path contains a line of
              FILE. The private overlay generates FILE from its own field
              names; it is never committed here. A hit in a generated pack
              is waived when a cited upstream source holds the same needle
              (below).

How text is matched (phrase and needle):
  - Case is ignored, Unicode is compared in one normal form (NFKC, case
    folded), and JSON `\\uXXXX` escapes are read as the characters they name.
  - A needle is a sequence of words: runs of letters and digits. A needle of
    several words matches the same words in the same order when what stands
    between two of them is spaces and tabs with at most one line break, the
    next line perhaps beginning with the comment or quote mark of a wrapped
    line (`#`, `//`, `*`, `>`, `;`, `%`, `|`); or exactly what stands between
    those two words in the needle itself. So `track number` matches `track
    number` and the two words on either side of a line break, and does not
    match `track_number` or `track-number`; a needle written `track_number`
    matches `track_number`.
  - A needle matches whole words only. An identifier is also read as its
    parts, so the needle `track` matches inside `trackNumber`, `TrackNumber`
    and `track_number`, and never inside `tracking`.
  - In scripts written without spaces between words, each character is a word,
    so a needle matches anywhere in a run of them.
  - The words of a marking may be separated by spaces, hyphens, underscores
    and line breaks, and by the comment or quote mark that starts a wrapped
    line.

  - Two needles that differ only in what stands between their words (`a_b`,
    `a-b`) are two needles. When the list is loaded, each needle is matched
    against itself alone on a line, and one that is not found is a finding.

The needle file holds one needle per line; blank lines are ignored. A needle
shorter than 4 characters, or with no letter or digit, is a finding that names
its line number. No output ever holds the text of a needle: a finding names
the needle's line number in FILE and its length.

A line may carry `boundary-allow: <reason>` to exempt it from the phrase and
hygiene checks only. A marking that spans lines is exempt only when every line
it touches carries the tag. No tag exempts a needle.

Generated packs (the needle waiver):
  A generated public pack restates names from a public upstream schema, and
  such a name can also be a needle. A needle hit in a generated pack is waived
  when the same needle matches, with the same matcher, in an upstream source
  file the pack's provenance cites, and that file's SHA-256 is the recorded
  one. Each waived hit is printed; none is silent. Precisely:
  - Generated pack: packs/<name>/ whose pack.def opens with comment lines
    that say `Generated by tools/gen/<generator>`, where that generator is a
    file of the tree. Packs named `conformance*` and `demo-*` are synthetic
    and never generated packs.
  - Files covered: every file under packs/<name>/ of a generated pack, and a
    module model/<m>.def whose opening comment says `Generated by` or
    `Last extended ... by` the same generator, and which a generated pack of
    that generator `uses` (the sources of all such packs count for it). File
    paths, and every other file, are never waived.
  - Provenance files: packs/<name>/PROVENANCE.md, and each file directly in
    tools/gen/sources/ whose name the pack's pack.def, pack.md or
    PROVENANCE.md, its generator, or a `tools/gen/*-run.sh` or `-run.py` the
    pack's files name, mentions.
  - Records: a SHA-256 is 64 hexadecimal digits. On a line `<sha256> <path>`
    the path is the rest of the line. Otherwise the names that go with it are
    the code spans, the table cells of one word and the last segment of each
    web address on its table row or line; when that row or line has no code
    span and no web address, those of its whole table or paragraph as well.
    An absolute path, `..`, a backslash and a web address are dropped.
  - Sources: looked for under each directory of GOAT_GEN_SOURCES (separated
    by os.pathsep) and then under tools/gen/sources/ of the tree, which git
    ignores. A record's path P is tried as <dir>/P, <dir>/<pack name>/P and,
    for a record in tools/gen/sources/<family>-PROVENANCE.md, <dir>/<family>/P.
    A file counts only when it is there and its SHA-256 is the recorded one;
    nothing is fetched. A file that is not UTF-8 text holds no needle.
  - A needle hit in a covered file whose sources are not all present stays a
    finding, and says which provenance files cite them and how many are
    present. The exit status does not count waived hits.

Usage:
  tools/boundary-gate [--root DIR] [--needles FILE] [--require-needles]
                      [--commits BASE..TIP] [--tag TAG] [--ref-name NAME]
                      [--needles-only | --example-overlay PREFIX --core DIR]

--example-overlay is for the public repository that shows an overlay with
invented formats: the names that begin with PREFIX must be marked as an overlay
marks them, and modules may come from the tree DIR. Nothing else is relaxed.

--commits also checks what each commit of a push publishes: its message, author
and committer, every path it changes and every content it brings in, also when
a later commit removes it. --tag checks an annotated tag, and --ref-name the
name of a ref. --needles-only runs the needle check alone, on the
tree of any repository.

After the findings, a line counts the waived hits, and one line for each
names the file, the line, and the needle's line number and length.

Exit status: 0 no finding (waived hits included), 1 one or more findings, 2 a
wrong command line.
"""
